+# Privacy by design: The Daily Grind
+
+## Third-party audit
+
+| Resource | Before | Now |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Hero photo | Loaded from `images.unsplash.com` on every page view. This sent each visitor's IP address, user agent, and referrer to a third party. | Self-hosted at `/images/hero.jpg` (Unsplash License, photo `1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085`). |
+| Fonts | System font stack | No change. No font CDN. |
+| Scripts | None | `/js/privacy.js` (first-party, no dependencies) |
+| Analytics / ads / social SDKs | None | None. The CSP blocks any added later until the policy is changed on purpose. |
+
+**Result:** the site makes zero third-party requests. CSP `default-src 'none'` with first-party-only allowlists enforces this in the browser.
+
+Re-audit before adding any third party, and at least once a quarter:
+1. Open DevTools > Network, load each page, and filter by "Domain" for any origin that isn't ours.
+2. Check DevTools > Issues for CSP or Permissions-Policy violations.
+3. Review reports arriving at `/api/csp-reports`.
+4. If a third party is unavoidable:
+   - Pin the URL to a version and add `integrity`, `crossorigin="anonymous"` and `referrerpolicy="no-referrer"`.
+   - For video or map embeds, use a click-to-load facade.
+   - Add the origin to the narrowest CSP directive possible.
+
+## Data minimization
+
+- The home page collects no personal data and sets no cookies.
+- `/js/privacy.js` removes campaign/ad-click identifiers (`utm_*`, `fbclid`, `gclid`, …) from the address bar, so they don't persist in history, bookmarks, or shared links.
+- The privacy notice in the footer states in plain language what we collect. Users don't have to read the full policy to find out.
+- Powerful browser features (camera, mic, geolocation, sensors, payment, Topics API, …) are disabled through `Permissions-Policy`. If the Locations page needs "near me", re-enable it on that route only (`geolocation=(self)`), and ask only after the user clicks.
+
+## Server-side requirements
+
+Static markup can't enforce these, so the backend must:
+
+- **Session cookie:** `Set-Cookie: __Host-session=…; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/`. Never store tokens or PII in `localStorage`.
+- **Logout:** accept `POST /logout` only (the nav uses a form). The response must send `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage"` (configured in `_headers`). Treat GET as a no-op so link prefetchers can't log users out.
+- **Account pages:** `Cache-Control: no-store` (configured in `_headers`).
+- **Data rights:** `/account/privacy` must let users view, export, correct, and delete their data with no support ticket.
+- **Logs and CSP reports:** strip emails, tokens, session IDs, and query strings before storing. Rate-limit account and lookup endpoints.
